    "answer": {
      "short": "$2673$",
      "worked": "Since $99=9\\times11$, the number must be divisible by both 9 and 11. Let the missing digits be $a$ and $b$, in that order.\n\nThe digit sum is $5+a+b$, between 5 and 23. Divisibility by 9 gives $a+b=4$ or $a+b=13$.\n\nThe alternating sum is $2-a+b-3=b-a-1$, between $-10$ and 8. Its only possible multiple of 11 is zero, so $b=a+1$.\n\nIf $a+b=4$, then $2a+1=4$, which gives no integer digit. For $a+b=13$:\n$$\\begin{align*}2a+1&=13\\\\2a&=12\\\\a&=6\\\\b&=7\\end{align*}$$\nThus the only number is $2673$. Check: $2673=99\\times27$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
